:"block","t":"\n            KTPH000KTPH001\n            \n  \n    About\n  \n\n          ","f":["<span class=\"md-nav__icon md-icon\">","</span>"]},{"s":31087,"e":31268,"k":"block","t":"\n      KTPH000\n        \n  \n  \n  KTPH001\n    \n  \n    Release notes\n  \n\n    \n  KTPH002\n  \n  \n\n      KTPH003\n    ","f":["<a href=\"../../about/release-notes.html\" class=\"md-nav__link\">","<span class=\"md-ellipsis\">","</span>","</a>"]},{"s":31379,"e":31560,"k":"block","t":"\n      KTPH000\n        \n  \n  \n  KTPH001\n    \n  \n    Feedback & support\n  \n\n    \n  KTPH002\n  \n  \n\n      KTPH003\n    ","f":["<a href=\"../../about/feedback.html\" class=\"md-nav__link\">","<span class=\"md-ellipsis\">","</span>","</a>"]},{"s":31671,"e":31883,"k":"block","t":"\n      KTPH000\n        \n  \n  \n  KTPH001\n    \n  \n    Anonymous data for application optimization\n  \n\n    \n  KTPH002\n  \n  \n\n      KTPH003\n    ","f":["<a href=\"../../about/anonymous-data.html\" class=\"md-nav__link\">","<span class=\"md-ellipsis\">","</span>","</a>"]},{"s":32387,"e":32404,"k":"attr","t":"Table of contents","f":[]},{"s":32736,"e":32816,"k":"block","t":"SecurityKTPH000","f":["<a class=\"headerlink\" href=\"#security\" title=\"Permanent link\">&para;</a>"]},{"s":32825,"e":33025,"k":"block","t":"This tab manages password vault security and SSH key authentication options. Open via KTPH001Configuration → Global Settings → SecurityKTPH002; stored in KTPH000.","f":["<code>~/.kortty/global-settings.xml</code>","<strong>","</strong>"]},{"s":33033,"e":33121,"k":"block","t":"KTPH000","f":["<img alt=\"Security settings tab\" src=\"../../assets/screenshots/settings/security.png\" />"]},{"s":33151,"e":33158,"k":"block","t":"Setting","f":[]},{"s":33168,"e":33172,"k":"block","t":"Type","f":[]},{"s":33182,"e":33188,"k":"block","t":"Values","f":[]},{"s":33198,"e":33205,"k":"block","t":"Default","f":[]},{"s":33215,"e":33224,"k":"block","t":"Stored as","f":[]},{"s":33262,"e":33284,"k":"block","t":"Change Master Password","f":[]},{"s":33294,"e":33300,"k":"block","t":"button","f":[]},{"s":33354,"e":33388,"k":"block","t":"Require master password on startup","f":[]},{"s":33398,"e":33404,"k":"block","t":"toggle","f":[]},{"s":33425,"e":33427,"k":"block","t":"On","f":[]},{"s":33437,"e":33480,"k":"block","t":"KTPH000","f":["<code>requireMasterPasswordOnStartup</code>"]},{"s":33501,"e":33555,"k":"block","t":"Disable master password prompt on startup (auto-login)","f":[]},{"s":33565,"e":33571,"k":"block","t":"toggle","f":[]},{"s":33592,"e":33595,"k":"block","t":"Off","f":[]},{"s":33605,"e":33642,"k":"block","t":"KTPH000","f":["<code>skipMasterPasswordPrompt</code>"]},{"s":33663,"e":33694,"k":"block","t":"Enable temporary SSH key option","f":[]},{"s":33704,"e":33710,"k":"block","t":"toggle","f":[]},{"s":33731,"e":33734,"k":"block","t":"Off","f":[]},{"s":33744,"e":33779,"k":"block","t":"KTPH000","f":["<code>temporarySshKeyEnabled</code>"]},{"s":33870,"e":33896,"k":"block","t":"Master password on startup","f":[]},{"s":33904,"e":34142,"k":"block","t":"If \"Require master password on startup\" is disabled, encrypted passwords and SSH keys cannot be automatically decrypted without manual password entry. This is a security risk and should only be disabled if you understand the consequences.","f":[]},{"s":34214,"e":34260,"k":"block","t":"Auto-login stores your master password on disk","f":[]},{"s":34268,"e":35384,"k":"block","t":"Enabling \"Disable master password prompt on startup\" makes korTTY remember your master password — stored only obfuscated (not securely encrypted) in KTPH000 with owner-only file permissions — and unlock the vault automatically on every start, with no dialog. Unlike disabling the option above, encrypted data (AI profiles, SSH passwords, credentials) stays usable, because the vault is actually unlocked. The trade-off: anyone who can read your KTPH001 folder or a backup can then decrypt KTPH002allKTPH003 saved passwords, SSH keys and API keys — the file permissions are the only remaining protection. On a brand-new profile korTTY sets up a default password automatically, so the app can start with no input at all. korTTY asks you to confirm before enabling it, and it is intended only for throwaway/test environments such as a VM. While auto-login is enabled, KTPH004Require master password on startupKTPH005 is switched off and greyed out — the two options are mutually exclusive. An enterprise policy that requires a master password overrides this option.","f":["<code>~/.kortty/master.autounlock</code>","<code>~/.kortty</code>","<strong>","</strong>","<strong>","</strong>"]},{"s":35453,"e":35500,"k":"block","t":"Unattended first launch (automation / test VMs)","f":[]},{"s":35508,"e":35716,"k":"block","t":"To bring korTTY up with no dialog at all — even on a brand-new profile that has never been unlocked — create KTPH000 before the first launch with the option already enabled:","f":["<code>~/.kortty/global-settings.xml</code>"]},{"s":36558,"e":37013,"k":"block","t":"On that first start korTTY sets up the default master password KTPH000 automatically and unlocks the vault, so scripted or CI runs need no interaction. If you later turn auto-login off on such a profile, KTPH001 is the password to enter — change it immediately via KTPH002Change Master PasswordKTPH003 if the profile is meant to live on. Intended for disposable/test environments only — see the security warning above.","f":["<code>kortty-auto</code>","<code>kortty-auto</code>","<strong>","</strong>"]},{"s":37083,"e":37112,"k":"block","t":"Change Master Password button","f":[]},{"s":37120,"e":37896,"k":"block","t":"Opens a dialog asking for the KTPH000Current PasswordKTPH001, the KTPH002New Password (at least 6 characters)KTPH003 and a KTPH004Confirm New PasswordKTPH005 repetition; KTPH006ChangeKTPH007 applies it. korTTY rejects a wrong current password, a new password under six characters, and a mismatch between the two new entries, each with its own message. On success it reports how many secrets were re-encrypted: every master-password-protected secret is decrypted with the old password and re-encrypted with the new one — connection passwords and key passphrases, jump-server passwords, SSH-key passphrases, stored credentials, AI-profile API keys and the other AI/translation keys, and RAG and Job Scheduler secrets — so nothing becomes unreadable after the change.","f":["<strong>","</strong>","<strong>","</strong>","<strong>","</strong>","<strong>","</strong>"]},{"s":37904,"e":38548,"k":"block","t":"The new password only takes over once every store has been migrated, so an error part-way through leaves the old password in effect instead of locking you out of half your data. If an individual secret cannot be migrated (for example one that was encrypted with a different password), korTTY leaves that value untouched, reports how many items were affected, and notes them in the log — those secrets keep the old password and must be re-entered manually. With auto-login enabled, the remembered password in KTPH000 is rewritten to the new master password, so automatic unlocking keeps working after the change.","f":["<code>~/.kortty/master.autounlock</code>"]},{"s":39433,"e":39448,"k":"text","t":"\n  Back to top\n","f":[]},{"s":39691,"e":39732,"k":"text","t":"MIT-licensed · Developed by Daniel Mengel","f":[]},{"s":39778,"e":39790,"k":"text","t":"\n    korTTY ","f":[]},{"s":39798,"e":39805,"k":"text","t":"v2.13.0","f":[]}]}